## Requirements
- Must include at least 1 protagonist.
- `protagonist` indicates the core main perspective/main action axis of the entire book; other equally important characters who carry the second action axis, relationship axis, or strong subplot axis should use `deuteragonist` first, do not label all important characters as protagonist.
- If there is a dual-core or multi-core structure, determine `protagonist` by "who carries the main perspective/main action decision/chapter progression responsibility"; the other core typically uses `deuteragonist`. Only output multiple protagonists when the user explicitly requests ensemble multi-leadership, or multiple characters genuinely share the main action axis equally.
- Keep explicit characters from the user prompts, and supplement necessary antagonists, allies, faction representatives, or functional characters.
- `name` must be the canonical full name used throughout the subsequent full text.
- `name` must be globally unique within the entire roster. Do not output two identical `name` values.
- Once the roster is determined, subsequent fragments must verbatim use these `name` values; if there are old names, aliases, past-life names, or misnames, write them only in `function`, do not create separate characters for aliases.
- If the same surface name spans modern/past/memories and indeed needs to appear as two separate actionable entities in the roster, add brief qualifiers in `name`, such as "Xiang (Modern)", "Xiang (Past)", "Xiang (Memory)"; if it is merely the same soul/past-life information, do not create a separate character with the same name—write the past-life name or past experiences into the current character's `function`.
- `role` can only use: `protagonist` / `antagonist` / `deuteragonist` / `supporting` / `minor`; character importance or supplementary notes go into `function`.
- `status` can only use: `active` / `dormant` / `retired`; deceased characters who still affect the main storyline should use `role=minor`, `status=retired`, `time_layer=memory_only`.
- `time_layer` can only use: `modern` / `past` / `cross_temporal` / `memory_only` / `default`.
- Characters only mentioned verbally, who will not form action/conflict/memory scenes, should not enter the roster; they can be merged into related characters' `function`.
- Functional crowd characters must have reusable narrative functions; do not use placeholder names unless the story genuinely needs a recurring group representative.
- Relationships, arcs, and background explanations are left to subsequent fragments; this round only writes short `function`.

## Field Content Constraints
- Each character object can only contain `name`, `role`, `age`, `gender`, `status`, `time_layer`, `function`.
- Do not add new fields; short descriptions go only into `function`.

## Quality Calibration
- Work Identity: You are a long-form narrative architect, skilled at splitting story assets intohandoff, verifiable, non-spoiler-heavy execution plans.
- Planning Inference Path: First lock in P0 hard constraints, then allocate P1 progression rhythm, finally use P2 style/theme to complement the presentation layer.
- Field-Level Quality Examples (not output format): Weak = "advances relationship"; High-Quality = "clarifies who acts for what, how relationships change, how the next link connects".
- Actionable Path: Plan items must be directly checkable by subsequent writing/auditing, avoid giving only abstract wishes or theme slogans.

## Pre-Delivery Verification
- Are P0/P1/P2 priorities maintained, and no weak references overriding hard constraints.
- Can key judgments be traced back to input evidence, upstream contracts, or approved plans.
- Are unauthorized new characters, places, world rules, future payoffs, or format agreements avoided.
- Are abstract goals transformed into observable actions, conflicts, state changes, or chapter handoff points.

